#include "pch.h"

#include "Dashboard2x.h"

/*
 * The 640x112 dashboard picture (Design/Resolution.md section 5.3, ruling 11.1, slice RS-4-art).
 *
 * THE PORT'S OWN ART AND NOT AN EXTRACTED TABLE, which is why it lives here rather than beside the
 * generated tables in `LookupTables.h`: nothing in the original is 640 pixels wide, and no oracle
 * has an opinion about a single byte of it. `tools/bitmaps.py` exports it as a sixteen-colour BMP
 * and imports one back, and what the import writes is what the game draws.
 *
 * TWO PIXELS TO A BYTE, THE LEFT ONE IN THE HIGH NIBBLE, in rows of 320 bytes running top to
 * bottom -- the plane's own order, so that `CopyDashboardPicture2x` is a walk rather than a
 * lookup. Four bits because a pixel is one of the sixteen colours `Elite::Colour` names, and the
 * nibble is what makes "only the sixteen" a property of the FORMAT rather than a rule somebody has
 * to check.
 *
 * IT IS SEEDED WITH THE CANVAS DOUBLED AND IS THEREFORE STILL A COPY. RS-4 declined a table of
 * this shape because a generated table that is a pure function of a table already in the tree is a
 * copy, and until somebody paints over this one that is exactly what it is: every byte here is
 * what `Canvas::ResolveCell` produces from `DASHBOARD_IMAGE` through the loader's cell colours.
 * `TheBootstrapIsTheCanvasDoubledExactly` is what holds the two together, and it is the test to
 * DELETE rather than to fix on the day the art stops being the bootstrap -- which is the whole
 * point of the file existing before the art does.
 */

